    "date_str": "2017-06-27",
    "description": "Long Term Solutions for the Management of Municipal Solid Waste (MSW) generated in the Western Region - Project for the Sanitary Waste  Landfill at Aruwakkalu in Puttalam",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=7871",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2017-06-27",
    "decision_details_body": "- As a long term solution for the management of Solid Waste of around 1200 MT collected daily in the Metro Colombo, it has been planned to transport the Solid Waste in 20ft containers with the necessary compaction by rail transportation and safe disposal from Kelaniya to the Aruwakkalu Sanitary Landfill to be constructed using the limestone pits which have been abandoned after excavation. Action is being taken to obtain the preliminary environmental investigation reports of the Central Environmental Authority and the Wayamba Provincial Environmental Authority and it is anticipated that the reports will be released by the end of July.  Subsequently, the operational activities of the project is expected to be commenced within 06 to 08 months. \n\nThe World Bank has agreed to provide the funds necessary for this project.  Accordingly, the proposal made by Hon. Patali Champika Ranawaka, the Minister of Megapolis and Western Development, to call for Limited International Competitive Bids and Limited National Competitive Bids in order to select a suitable contractor in accordance with the National Procurement Guidelines to commence the project expeditiously, to acquire the extent of land required for the proposed project from the land at Aruwakkalu owned by the Sri Lanka Cement Corporation, to empower the Standing Cabinet Appointed Procurement Committee to conduct negotiations with the Sri Lanka Cement Corporation, Siam City Cement (Lanka) Pvt Ltd. including the public and private sector stakeholders to obtain the other facilities needed in matters related to its operational activities, acquisition of lands and properties necessary for construction, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2017-06-27-025-en",
    "num": "25",
    "date_str": "2017-06-27",
    "description": "Handing over of the Dr. Neville Fernando Sri Lanka Russia Friendship Hospital to the Government and the proposal for the Future Management Structure of the South Asian Institute of Technology and Medicine (SAITM)",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=7870",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2017-06-27",
    "decision_details_body": "- Although, the Dr. Nevile Fernando Sri Lanka Rusia Friendship Hospital which was established in 2013 to function as a Private Hospital with 500 beds and room facilities with 100 beds achieved a gradual development providing treatment for around 3000 Outdoor patients per month and about 100 Indoor patients, in the face of the present state of affairs, it has been difficult to attract an adequate number of Indoor patients to the hospital required to provide clinical training for Medical Students.  \n\nTaking into consideration, the availability of only a limited number of institutions which provide tertiary health care in the East Colombo area and it being situated in close proximity to the Expressway,the joint proposal made by Hon. (Dr. Rajitha Senaratne, the Minister of Health, Nutrition and Indigenous Medicine and Hon. Lakshman Kiriella, the Minister of Higher Education and Highways to get the Dr. Nevile Fernando Hospital worth of a government valuation of Rs.3.55 billion vested with the government of Sri Lanka, to transfer its full ownership to the government after the settlement of the loan obtained from the Bank of Ceylon for the establishment of SAITM, to provide free medical health care services to the public and to appoint a Governing Board by the Ministry of Health, Nutrition and Indigenous Medicine for the management of the hospital similar in nature to the Administration Structure prevailing in the University of Jayawardenapura, was approved by the Cabinet.  Further, it was also decided that alternative proposals should be presented in consultation with all the related stakeholders to widen the ownership of the SAITM."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2017-06-27-024-en",
    "num": "24",
    "date_str": "2017-06-27",
    "description": "Approval of a  Service Minute Unique to the Postal Department",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=7869",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2017-06-27",
    "decision_details_body": "- Although, the Postal Department has got 9 Schemes of Recruitments approved for its Staff in accordance with the provisions of the Public Administration Circular No.06/2006, practical difficulties have arisen in the implementation of such Schemes of Recruitments and these difficulties are yet to be resolved.  Therefore, the proposal made by Hon. M.H.A. Haleem, the Minister of Posts, Postal Services and Muslim Religious Affairs, to appoint a Committee comprising of the representatives of the Ministries of Public Administration and Management, Finance and Mass Media, Posts, Postal Services and Muslim Religious Affairs and the National Salaries and Cadres Commission headed by a Senior Official named by the Secretary to the Prime Minister, to examine the possibility of preparing a Service Minute unique to the Postal Department comprising of Schemes of Recruitment for each Grade which could be easily implemented and make recommendations in that regard, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2017-06-27-023-en",
    "num": "23",
    "date_str": "2017-06-27",
    "description": "Lease of a Land situated at the Hospital Road, Thalapathpitiya for a Mixed Development Project",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=7868",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2017-06-27",
    "decision_details_body": "- Bids have been called for under the Government Procurement Procedure to allocate a part of the land in extent of 6 Acres 0 Roods 9.6 Perches situated at Battaramulla and Sri Jayawardenapura Kotte acquired for the Urban Development Authority under the Sri Lanka, Jayawardenapura Kotte, Parliament Development Project, to select a suitable developer for implementation of a Mixed Development Project.  In response, 3 bidders have tendered there bids and accordingly, the proposal made by Hon. Patali Champika Ranawaka, the Minister of Megapolis and Western Development, to lease the land to the Monarch Imperial (Pvt) Ltd., the bidder who has offered the highest bid relatively to the government valuation of Rs.514 million, for a sum of Rs.1,200 million on a 99 year lease basis, subject to the conditions stipulated, as per the recommendations of the Technical Committee appointed by the Cabinet to evaluate the bids and the Standing Cabinet Appointed Procurement Committee (SCAPC), was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2017-06-27-022-en",
    "num": "22",
    "date_str": "2017-06-27",
    "description": "Introduction of the Electronic Procurement System into the Public Procurement in Sri Lanka",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=7867",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2017-06-27",
    "decision_details_body": "- The procurement activities pertaining to the Public Sector forms a considerable part of the Sri Lanka's economy, since about 20% to 25% of the total Government spending is for the procurement of Goods and Services of the Public Sector. Under the present procurement process due to the shortcomings, such as persistent delay in the bid evaluation and the lack of transparency and competition, various problems crop up and this situation has a direct negative impact on the economic development of the country.  \n\nAt the 4th South Asia Region Public Procurement Conference held in Colombo and Kandy from February 20th to 23rd 2017 under the theme of 'Electronic Government Procurement in South Asia - Achievements, Opportunities and Challenges' where H.E. the President participated, it was revealed that except for Sri Lanka, most of the other South Asian countries use the Electronic Procurement System and have shown a remarkable progress. Since, the use of modern technology could minimize the deficiencies found in the procurement process, it is felt that the procurement process also could be re\u2011oriented accordingly by introducing an Electronic Procurement System.  \n\nAccordingly, the proposal made by Hon. Mangala Samaraweera, the Minister of Finance and Mass Media, to implement the Public Electronic Procurement System in the Public Procurement Process and to appoint an Officials' Committee under the Chairmanship of the Director General of the Department of Public Finance comprising of the representatives of other relevant institutions with the concurrence of the National Procurement Commission, with the responsibility for preparation of an Action Plan, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2017-06-27-021-en",
